OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026", "title": "The Factor-Two Hardness Threshold for Vertex Cover", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "102", "family_title": "The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds", "abstract_html": "We prove that minimum Vertex Cover is NP-hard to approximate within every fixed factor below two, even on simple unweighted graphs.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to graphs \\(G\\) such that …", "statement": "<p>The explicit cover gap (paper Theorem 1.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A graph is finite, simple, undirected, and unweighted.</p><p class=\"li\">For a graph \\(G\\), let \\(n=|V(G)|\\), and let \\(\\tau(G)\\) be the minimum cardinality of a vertex cover of \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A vertex cover meets every edge. Its complement is an independent set.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(m\\ge 4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to graphs \\(G\\) such that\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{the formula is satisfiable}\n&amp;\\Longrightarrow\n\\tau(G)&lt;\\left(\\frac12+\\frac1m\\right)|V(G)|,\\\\\n\\text{the formula is unsatisfiable}\n&amp;\\Longrightarrow\n\\tau(G)&gt;\\left(1-\\frac1m\\right)|V(G)|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe output lists all vertices and edges explicitly. The polynomial-time bound is for each fixed \\(m\\); its constants may depend on \\(m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(m\\ge4\\). Choose all constants before requesting the source alphabets:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na&amp;=\\frac1{4m},\n&amp;\nb_0&amp;=\\frac1{2m},\n&amp;\n\\nu&amp;=\\frac1{2^7m^3},\\\\\n\\beta&amp;=\\frac1{2^{10}m^3},\n&amp;\n\\ell&amp;=2^{11}m^3,\n&amp;\nh&amp;=2^{12}m^3,\\\\\nd&amp;=2^{38}m^{10},\n&amp;\n\\sigma&amp;=\\frac1{2^{46}m^{12}},\n&amp;\np&amp;=2d+1,\\\\\nt&amp;=2^{17}m^4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThese are the joint parameter choices in Section 8 of the paper.</p><p>The construction conditions hold because \\(d\\) is even and\n\\[\n\\sqrt d=2^{19}m^5&gt;4m.\n\\]\nAlso \\(t=a\\sqrt d\\).</p><p>For the restriction conditions, \\(h&lt;d\\), and\n\\[\n16h+32h^2\n=\n2^{16}m^3+2^{29}m^6\n\\le\n2^{30}m^7\n=\n\\frac{\\nu d}{2}.\n\\]\nThe displayed inequality holds already for \\(m\\ge1\\).</p><p>The remaining list and decoding identities are\n\\[\n\\frac{\\beta h}{2}=2,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma=\\frac1{h^2\\ell^2},\n\\qquad\n\\binom h2\\ell^2\\sigma\n=\n\\frac{h-1}{2h}\n&lt;\n\\frac12.\n\\]</p><p>All choices depend only on \\(m\\); no label alphabet has entered them. Their magnitude affects the constants and degree of the polynomial running time, but every fixed \\(m\\) gives a fixed reduction.</p><p>Apply the source promise in Proposition 1 at this \\(\\sigma\\) to obtain fixed source alphabets and a polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\). Now define\n\\[\nq=\\max\\{|\\Sigma_U|,|\\Sigma_V|\\},\n\\qquad\nr=q^{d-1},\n\\]\nand construct the graph.</p><p>Proposition 1 makes its vertex and edge lists explicit in deterministic polynomial time. All hypotheses of Propositions 2 and 7 have been verified. A satisfiable formula yields source value \\(1\\), and Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n\\tau(G)&lt;\\left(\\frac12+\\frac1m\\right)|V(G)|.\n\\]\nAn unsatisfiable formula yields source value at most \\(\\sigma\\), and Proposition 7 gives\n\\[\n\\tau(G)&gt;\\left(1-\\frac1m\\right)|V(G)|.\n\\]</p><p>The construction enumerates all finite seeds and grid weights. Random variables describe the uniform distribution on this output and are used only in its analysis. Neither the independent set, the function \\(F\\), nor the conditional means need to be computed by the reduction.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Approximating Vertex Cover within factor \\(\\alpha\\) is NP-hard.", "statement": "<p>The factor-two approximation threshold (paper Corollary 1.2).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a finite simple undirected unweighted graph \\(G\\), let \\(\\tau(G)\\) denote its minimum vertex-cover cardinality.</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(\\alpha\\)-approximation returns an actual vertex cover of cardinality at most \\(\\alpha\\tau(G)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(1\\le \\alpha&lt;2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Approximating Vertex Cover within factor \\(\\alpha\\) is NP-hard. Consequently, unless \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\), no deterministic polynomial-time algorithm achieves factor \\(2-\\varepsilon\\) for any fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\in(0,1]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\alpha\\in[1,2)\\). Choose a fixed integer \\(m\\ge4\\) such that\n\\[\n\\alpha\n&lt;\nR_m\n:=\n\\frac{1-1/m}{1/2+1/m}\n=\n2-\\frac6{m+2}.\n\\]\nSuch a choice exists because \\(R_m\\) tends to \\(2\\).</p><p>Suppose a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm always returns a vertex cover of cardinality at most \\(\\alpha\\tau(G)\\). Apply the reduction in Theorem 1.</p><p>On a completeness instance, the algorithm’s output has cardinality strictly less than\n\\[\n\\alpha\\left(\\frac12+\\frac1m\\right)n\n&lt;\n\\left(1-\\frac1m\\right)n.\n\\]\nOn a soundness instance, every cover has cardinality strictly greater than\n\\[\n\\left(1-\\frac1m\\right)n.\n\\]\nComparing the output cardinality with this rational threshold distinguishes the two source cases.</p><p>The comparison uses only integer arithmetic: multiply the output cardinality by \\(m\\) and compare with \\((m-1)n\\). The real number \\(\\alpha\\) is used only to choose the fixed integer \\(m\\); it is not an input to the reduction. This proves the NP-hardness assertion and the stated consequence unless \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\).</p><p>For completeness, the matching upper bound on the approximation factor is elementary. Find a maximal matching and return all its endpoints. Maximality makes this set a vertex cover: an edge with neither endpoint selected could be added to the matching. Every vertex cover contains an endpoint of each matching edge, and these edges are disjoint. The returned cover therefore has cardinality at most twice optimum.</p><p>Together with the hardness assertion, this gives the constant approximation threshold \\(2\\), unless \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every fixed rational \\(\\sigma\\in(0,1)\\), there are nonempty constant source alphabets and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to Label Cover instances of the above form such that …", "statement": "<p>The source promise, compatibility norm, and deterministic graph construction (paper Theorem 2.1, Lemmas 3.1–3.2, and Proposition 3.3).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>The source problem:</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\([k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Label Cover instance \\(\\Phi\\) consists of disjoint finite variable sets \\(U,V\\), nonempty finite alphabets \\(\\Sigma_U,\\Sigma_V\\), and an explicit nonempty list of \\(M\\) constraint occurrences.</p><p class=\"li\">An occurrence \\(c\\in[M]\\) has endpoints \\(x_c\\in U\\), \\(y_c\\in V\\), and a total map\n\\[\n\\pi_c:\\Sigma_U\\longrightarrow\\Sigma_V.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A labeling \\(A\\) assigns to each variable a symbol from its side’s alphabet. It satisfies occurrence \\(c\\) when\n\\[\n\\pi_c(A(x_c))=A(y_c).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">The value \\(\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)\\) is the maximum fraction of satisfied occurrences. List entries count with their multiplicity.</p><p class=\"li\">Endpoints and projection-map tables are given explicitly in binary. Unused variable declarations may be removed. No regularity or expansion condition is required.</p><p>Construction parameters:</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an integer \\(m\\ge4\\), fixed source alphabets, and an even square integer \\(d\\) satisfying\n\\[\nd\\ge4,\\qquad \\sqrt d&gt;4m.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\na=\\frac1{4m},\\qquad p=2d+1,\\qquad t=a\\sqrt d,\n\\qquad q=\\max\\{|\\Sigma_U|,|\\Sigma_V|\\},\\qquad r=q^{d-1}.\n\\]\nThen \\(p,\\sqrt d\\) are integers, \\(t\\) is rational, and\n\\[\n\\frac d p&lt;\\frac12&lt;\\frac t2,\\qquad p&gt;2m.\n\\]</p><p>Queries and labels:</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal E=\\binom{[d]}2.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A query of type \\(j\\in[d]\\) has the form\n\\[\ni=\\bigl(j,(q_{jk})_{k\\in[d]\\setminus\\{j\\}}\\bigr),\n\\qquad\nq_{jk}\\in\n\\begin{cases}\nV,&amp;k&lt;j,\\\\\nU,&amp;k&gt;j,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwith the tuple ordered by increasing \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q\\) be the set of all such queries of all types. Let \\(P(i)\\subseteq U\\cup V\\) be the set of distinct variables appearing in \\(i\\). Repeated entries contribute only one variable, so \\(|P(i)|\\le d-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A label \\(\\lambda\\) for \\(i\\) assigns a symbol from the appropriate alphabet to every variable in \\(P(i)\\) and satisfies every source constraint occurrence whose two endpoints belong to \\(P(i)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Lambda_i\\) be the set of these labels; it may be empty. There are at most \\(q^{|P(i)|}\\le r\\) labels.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix orders on variables and alphabet symbols. List valid assignments lexicographically to define an injection\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slot}_i:\\Lambda_i\\longrightarrow[r].\n\\]\nFor \\(\\Lambda_i=\\varnothing\\), this is the empty map.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the finite coordinate set\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(i,\\lambda):i\\in Q,\\ \\lambda\\in\\Lambda_i\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A compatible selection is a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\mathcal P\\) with at most one label per query, such that its labels agree on overlaps and their induced labeling satisfies every source constraint whose two endpoints lie in\n\\[\nP(I)=\\bigcup_{(i,\\lambda)\\in I}P(i).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the family of compatible selections, including the empty selection. No extension to a satisfying labeling outside \\(P(I)\\) is required.</p><p>Norm and increments:</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z\\in\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal P}\\), define\n\\[\n\\|z\\|_*=\n\\max_{I\\in\\mathcal I}\n\\left|\\sum_{(i,\\lambda)\\in I}z_{i,\\lambda}\\right|.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a query \\(i\\) and \\(s\\in\\mathbb R^r\\), define \\(z(i,s)\\) to be supported on the \\(i\\)-block, with\n\\[\nz(i,s)_{i,\\lambda}=s_{\\operatorname{slot}_i(\\lambda)}\n\\qquad(\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_i).\n\\]\nAll other coordinates are zero.</p><p>Vertices and edges:</p><p class=\"li\">A seed tuple is \\(\\mathbf c=(c_e)_{e\\in\\mathcal E}\\in[M]^{\\mathcal E}\\). For \\(j&lt;k\\), it supplies\n\\[\nq_{jk}=x_{c_{\\{j,k\\}}},\\qquad\nq_{kj}=y_{c_{\\{j,k\\}}}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(i_j(\\mathbf c)\\) for the resulting query of type \\(j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A query retains its type and ordered endpoint questions. It does not retain sampled occurrence indices, sampled projection maps, or opposite endpoints.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nT=\\left\\{\\frac{2u}{p}:u=-d,-d+1,\\ldots,d\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThese are the midpoints of the \\(p\\) equal consecutive subintervals of \\([-1,1]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The vertices are all indexed pairs\n\\[\nv=(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf s),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf c\\in[M]^{\\mathcal E},\\quad\n\\mathbf s=(s_1,\\ldots,s_d)\\in(T^r)^d.\n\\]\nDifferent indexed pairs remain distinct vertices even when they produce the same queries or the same vector\n\\[\nS_v=\\sum_{j=1}^d z(i_j(\\mathbf c),s_j).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For distinct vertices \\(v,w\\), declare\n\\[\n\\{v,w\\}\\in E(G)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\|S_v+S_w\\|_*\\le2t.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. For every fixed rational \\(\\sigma\\in(0,1)\\), there are nonempty constant source alphabets and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to Label Cover instances of the above form such that satisfiable formulas give value \\(1\\), and unsatisfiable formulas give value at most \\(\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The family \\(\\mathcal I\\) is hereditary and contains every coordinate singleton. The displayed formula defines a norm, and\n\\[\n\\|z(i,s)\\|_*\\le\\|s\\|_\\infty.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The graph \\(G\\) can be output explicitly in deterministic time polynomial in the source bit length, for fixed \\(m,d\\), and source alphabets. Every edge test is exact.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Its order is\n\\[\nn=|V(G)|=M^{\\binom d2}p^{rd}\\ge p&gt;2m.\n\\]\nChoosing a uniform vertex is exactly choosing all occurrence seeds independently and uniformly from \\([M]\\), and all weight entries independently and uniformly from \\(T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The source-hardness assertion is Lemma 1.1. The norm assertions are Lemma 1.2. The vertex and edge definitions give a finite simple undirected unweighted graph. Its order is\n\\[\nn=M^{\\binom d2}p^{rd}\\ge p&gt;2m,\n\\]\nusing \\(M,r\\ge1\\) and the construction conditions. The uniform-vertex law follows directly from enumeration of all indexed seeds and grid weights, with no identification of equal vectors.</p><p>For the deterministic polynomial construction, let \\(L\\) be the source bit length. The fixed integers \\(m,d\\) determine \\(p\\) and the rational number \\(t\\). The fixed alphabets determine \\(q\\) and \\(r=q^{d-1}\\). All these quantities are therefore computable constants independent of \\(L\\).</p><p>Since the constraint list is explicit, \\(M=O(L)\\), and\n\\[\nn=O\\!\\left(L^{\\binom d2}\\right).\n\\]\nA vertex description contains a fixed number of source indices and grid entries. Thus all vertices can be enumerated and assigned identifiers of \\(O(1+\\log n)\\) bits.</p><p>Apply Lemma 1.3 to each unordered pair of distinct vertices. All label and selection enumerations have a fixed number of choices. Variable comparisons, sorting, constraint scans, and projection-map evaluations take polynomial bit time in \\(L\\).</p><p>The arithmetic of the edge test uses only rational grid entries. Every coordinate of \\(S_v+S_w\\) has denominator \\(p\\), after summing all repeated-block contributions. The local maximum is therefore \\(\\zeta/p\\) for a nonnegative integer \\(\\zeta\\). By the increment bound and the triangle inequality,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\zeta}{p}\\le2d,\n\\]\nso its numerator and denominator have bit lengths bounded solely in terms of the fixed parameters. Since \\(t=\\sqrt d/(4m)\\) and \\(\\sqrt d\\) is an integer, the edge test is precisely\n\\[\n2m\\zeta\\le p\\sqrt d.\n\\]\nBoth sides are integers, so the test involves no approximation of a real number.</p><p>Checking all pairs and writing every qualifying edge takes\n\\[\nO\\!\\left((1+n^2)\\operatorname{poly}(L+\\log n)\\right)\n\\]\nbit operations, with constants depending only on the fixed parameters and alphabets. The formula for \\(n\\) also computes \\(n\\) by integer arithmetic with \\(O(1+\\log n)\\) output bits. Thus the construction has polynomial bit complexity, including its explicit vertex and edge lists. It runs on every source instance with these alphabets, regardless of whether the gap promise holds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are nonempty constant alphabets and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to such Label Cover instances \\(\\Phi\\) satisfying …", "statement": "<p>Perfect-completeness Label Cover hardness (paper Theorem 2.1; the external hardness input).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the Label Cover formulation in Proposition 1: an explicit nonempty unweighted list of total projection constraints, with list multiplicities counted.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a rational \\(\\sigma\\in(0,1)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are nonempty constant alphabets and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(3\\mathrm{SAT}\\) to such Label Cover instances \\(\\Phi\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{the formula is satisfiable}\n&amp;\\Longrightarrow \\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)=1,\\\\\n\\text{the formula is unsatisfiable}\n&amp;\\Longrightarrow \\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)\\le\\sigma.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe alphabets may depend on \\(\\sigma\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the paper’s quoted external hardness input, not a new hardness theorem proved in the paper. It is the standard consequence of the PCP theorem of Arora–Safra and Arora–Lund–Motwani–Sudan–Szegedy, together with Raz’s parallel repetition theorem.</p><p>A quantitative formulation appears in the full version of Dinur–Steurer, Theorem 6.2. The precise unweighted, total-projection formulation is recorded in Dinur–Khot–Kindler–Minzer–Safra, Definitions 1.1–1.2 and Theorem 1.3. There the constraints are even \\(D\\)-to-one for a suitable constant \\(D\\), a property not used here. The constants, including the alphabets and \\(D\\), may depend on \\(\\sigma\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The family \\(\\mathcal I\\) is hereditary and contains every coordinate singleton.", "statement": "<p>Compatibility norm (paper Lemma 3.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the queries \\(Q\\), local labels \\(\\Lambda_i\\), coordinate set \\(\\mathcal P\\), compatible selections \\(\\mathcal I\\), and increments \\(z(i,s)\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z\\in\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal P}\\), put\n\\[\n\\|z\\|_*=\n\\max_{I\\in\\mathcal I}\n\\left|\\sum_{(i,\\lambda)\\in I}z_{i,\\lambda}\\right|.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family \\(\\mathcal I\\) is hereditary and contains every coordinate singleton. The displayed formula defines a norm. For each fixed query \\(i\\), the increment is linear in \\(s\\) and obeys\n\\[\n\\|z(i,s)\\|_*\\le\\|s\\|_\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Removing labels preserves agreement and can only shrink the scope union. Every constraint internal to the smaller union was therefore satisfied before removal. This proves heredity. Every singleton belongs to \\(\\mathcal I\\) by the definition of a valid query label.</p><p>The maximum of absolute linear forms is absolutely homogeneous and satisfies the triangle inequality; the singleton forms ensure that it vanishes only at zero. If the coordinate set is empty, this is the unique norm on the zero-dimensional space.</p><p>Finally, a compatible selection contains at most one coordinate of the \\(i\\)-block, proving\n\\[\n\\|z(i,s)\\|_*\\le\\|s\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nLinearity follows from the coordinate formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\|z\\|_*= \\max_{\\substack{I\\in\\mathcal I:\\\\ (i,\\lambda)\\in I\\Rightarrow i\\in B}} \\left|\\sum_{(i,\\lambda)\\in I}z_{i,\\lambda}\\right|. \\) For \\(z=S_v+S_w\\), this maximum can be evaluated by at most \\((1+r)^{2d}\\) …", "statement": "<p>Local evaluation of the norm (paper Lemma 3.2).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the norm, graph vectors, queries, labels, and bound \\(r\\) from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\subseteq Q\\) be a set of query blocks supporting \\(z\\in\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal P}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\|z\\|_*=\n\\max_{\\substack{I\\in\\mathcal I:\\\\\n(i,\\lambda)\\in I\\Rightarrow i\\in B}}\n\\left|\\sum_{(i,\\lambda)\\in I}z_{i,\\lambda}\\right|.\n\\]\nFor \\(z=S_v+S_w\\), this maximum can be evaluated by at most \\((1+r)^{2d}\\) choices, each checked by a scan of the source constraints.</p>", "proof": "<p>Intersect any compatible selection with the blocks in \\(B\\). Heredity from Lemma 1.2 preserves compatibility, and the sum is unchanged because all other blocks of \\(z\\) vanish. Conversely, each selection in the restricted maximum is a selection in the full maximum. This proves equality.</p><p>For a pair of vertices there are at most \\(2d\\) distinct query blocks. First aggregate the coordinate contributions of all occurrences of each repeated query. For each distinct query \\(i\\), enumerate the at most \\(q^{d-1}\\le r\\) assignments on its scope, and keep exactly those satisfying every source constraint internal to that scope. This computes \\(\\Lambda_i\\) and its slots.</p><p>Next choose either no label or one of these labels in each block. There are at most \\((1+r)^{2d}\\) choices. For each choice, test agreement on repeated variables and scan the source list to test every constraint internal to the union of the chosen scopes. This union has at most \\(2d(d-1)\\) variables. The surviving choices are precisely the selections in the restricted maximum, and their absolute coordinate sums give the required maximum. Neither the labels nor the compatibility test requires satisfying any constraint outside the indicated scope union.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The graph \\(G\\) has an independent set of cardinality greater than \\( \\left(\\frac12-\\frac1m\\right)n. \\) Consequently, \\( \\tau(G)<\\left(\\frac12+\\frac1m\\right)n. \\)", "statement": "<p>Completeness (paper Proposition 3.5).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be the graph constructed in Proposition 1 from a source instance \\(\\Phi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Thus \\(m\\ge4\\), \\(d\\ge4\\) is an even square integer with \\(\\sqrt d&gt;4m\\),\n\\[\na=\\frac1{4m},\\qquad p=2d+1,\\qquad t=a\\sqrt d,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nT=\\left\\{\\frac{2u}{p}:u=-d,\\ldots,d\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(n=|V(G)|\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The graph \\(G\\) has an independent set of cardinality greater than\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac12-\\frac1m\\right)n.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\tau(G)&lt;\\left(\\frac12+\\frac1m\\right)n.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be a labeling satisfying every source constraint. Its restriction \\(\\lambda_{A,i}\\) to \\(P(i)\\) belongs to \\(\\Lambda_i\\) for every query \\(i\\). The selection\n\\[\nI_A=\\{(i,\\lambda_{A,i}):i\\in Q\\}\n\\]\nbelongs to \\(\\mathcal I\\): the labels agree on overlaps, and every constraint internal to their scope union is satisfied by \\(A\\).</p><p>Keep precisely the vertices for which\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^d\ns_{j,\\operatorname{slot}_{i_j(\\mathbf c)}\n(\\lambda_{A,i_j(\\mathbf c)})}\n&gt;t.\n\\]\nFor a uniform vertex, conditional on the seed tuple, the selected slot in each weight vector is fixed. Its entries are independent uniform elements of \\(T\\), one from each independent \\(s_j\\). Coincident endpoint questions or slot numbers do not change this independence. By Lemma 2.1, more than a \\((1/2-1/m)\\) fraction of vertices are kept.</p><p>For two distinct kept vertices \\(v,w\\), evaluating \\(S_v+S_w\\) on \\(I_A\\) gives exactly the sum of their two displayed expressions. This includes all contributions even when the same query appears in both vertices. The sum exceeds \\(2t\\), so the norm definition rules out an edge between \\(v\\) and \\(w\\).</p><p>The kept vertices are therefore independent. Their complement is a vertex cover of cardinality less than \\((1/2+1/m)n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\Pr\\!\\left[\\sum_{j=1}^dT_j>t\\right] > \\frac12-\\sqrt2\\left(a+\\frac1{\\sqrt d}\\right) > \\frac12-\\frac1m. \\)", "statement": "<p>Midpoint-grid tail (paper Lemma 3.4).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit \\(m,d,a,p,t,T\\) from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_1,\\ldots,T_d\\) be independent uniform elements of \\(T\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\Pr\\!\\left[\\sum_{j=1}^dT_j&gt;t\\right]\n&gt;\n\\frac12-\\sqrt2\\left(a+\\frac1{\\sqrt d}\\right)\n&gt;\n\\frac12-\\frac1m.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take independent uniform \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_d\\) on \\([-1,1]\\), and round each to the midpoint of its subinterval in the partition defining \\(T\\). Boundary choices have probability zero. The rounded variables have the required independent laws, and\n\\[\n\\left|\\sum_jT_j-\\sum_jC_j\\right|\n\\le\\frac d p&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\nA=t+\\frac d p=a\\sqrt d+\\frac d p.\n\\]\nWe bound the central probability\n\\[\n\\Pr\\!\\left[\\left|\\sum_jC_j\\right|\\le A\\right].\n\\]</p><p>Each \\(C_j\\) may be generated as \\((L_j+U_j)/2\\), where \\(L_j\\) is a fair sign and \\(U_j\\) is uniform on \\([-1,1]\\), all independent. The two sign choices give uniform distributions on the two half intervals of \\([-1,1]\\), so this is indeed the uniform distribution on the full interval.</p><p>Conditional on \\((U_1,\\ldots,U_d)\\), the event \\(\\left|\\sum_jC_j\\right|\\le A\\) places \\(\\sum_jL_j\\) in an interval of length\n\\[\n4A=4a\\sqrt d+\\frac{4d}{p}&lt;4a\\sqrt d+2.\n\\]\nThe possible sign sums are spaced by two. An interval of length \\(D\\) contains at most \\(D/2+1\\) values from such a lattice, so this interval contains fewer than \\(2a\\sqrt d+2\\) possible sign sums.</p><p>Write \\(d=2d_0\\). The sign-sum probabilities are\n\\[\n4^{-d_0}\\binom{2d_0}{v},\n\\qquad 0\\le v\\le2d_0.\n\\]\nConsecutive ratios \\((2d_0-v)/(v+1)\\) show that the largest is the central one. It satisfies\n\\[\n4^{-d_0}\\binom{2d_0}{d_0}\n=\n\\prod_{u=1}^{d_0}\\left(1-\\frac1{2u}\\right)\n\\le\\frac1{\\sqrt{d_0+1}}\n&lt;\n\\frac{\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt d}.\n\\]\nFor the middle inequality, square the product and use\n\\[\n\\left(1-\\frac1{2u}\\right)^2\\le\\frac u{u+1}\n\\qquad(u\\ge1).\n\\]\nIndeed the right side minus the left side is\n\\[\n\\frac{3u-1}{4u^2(u+1)}&gt;0,\n\\]\nand the resulting product telescopes.</p><p>The conditional central probability, and therefore its average, is bounded by\n\\[\n\\Pr\\!\\left[\\left|\\sum_jC_j\\right|\\le A\\right]\n&lt;\n\\sqrt2\\left(2a+\\frac2{\\sqrt d}\\right).\n\\]\nSymmetry gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr\\!\\left[\\sum_jC_j&gt;A\\right]\n&amp;=\n\\frac12\\left(\n1-\\Pr\\!\\left[\\left|\\sum_jC_j\\right|\\le A\\right]\n\\right)\\\\\n&amp;&gt;\n\\frac12-\\sqrt2\\left(a+\\frac1{\\sqrt d}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nOn this event, rounding leaves \\(\\sum_jT_j&gt;t\\).</p><p>Finally, \\(\\sqrt d&gt;4m=1/a\\), and hence\n\\[\n\\sqrt2\\left(a+\\frac1{\\sqrt d}\\right)\n&lt;\n2\\sqrt2\\,a\n&lt;\n3a\n=\n\\frac3{4m}\n&lt;\n\\frac1m.\n\\]\nThis proves both inequalities.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a fixed nonempty finite set \\(A_*\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal P}\\) such that \\( \\Pr_{v\\in V(G)}[S_v\\in A_*]\\ge b_0, \\qquad \\|x+y\\|_*>2t \\quad(x,y\\in A_*), \\) where \\(v\\) is uniform.", "statement": "<p>Separation, variance, and oscillation (paper Lemma 4.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\), \\(\\Phi\\), \\(\\mathcal P\\), \\(\\mathcal I\\), \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_*\\), \\(z(i,s)\\), \\(d,p,t\\), and \\(n=|V(G)|\\) be as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nb_0=\\frac1{2m},\n\\qquad\n\\nu=\\frac{b_0a^2}{4}=\\frac1{2^7m^3}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that an independent set contains at least \\(b_0n\\) vertices with \\(\\|S_v\\|_*&gt;t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonempty finite set \\(B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal P}\\), define\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_*(z,B)=\\min_{b\\in B}\\|z-b\\|_*.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed nonempty finite set \\(A_*\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal P}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Pr_{v\\in V(G)}[S_v\\in A_*]\\ge b_0,\n\\qquad\n\\|x+y\\|_*&gt;2t\n\\quad(x,y\\in A_*),\n\\]\nwhere \\(v\\) is uniform.</p><p>The function\n\\[\nF(z)=\\frac12\\left(\n\\operatorname{dist}_*(z,-A_*)-\\operatorname{dist}_*(z,A_*)\n\\right)\n\\]\nis odd and \\(1\\)-Lipschitz for \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_*\\), and \\(F(z)&gt;t\\) on \\(A_*\\).</p><p>Draw fresh independent uniform occurrence seeds\n\\[\n\\mathbf c=(c_e)_{e\\in\\binom{[d]}2}\\in[M]^{\\binom{[d]}2}\n\\]\nand independent weight blocks \\(s_j\\) uniform on \\([-1,1]^r\\), independently of the seeds. Put\n\\[\nf(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf s)\n=\nF\\!\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^d z(i_j(\\mathbf c),s_j)\\right).\n\\]\nThis bounded function satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf s}f(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf s)=0\n\\quad\\text{for every }\\mathbf c,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb Ef^2\\ge\\nu d.\n\\]</p><p>On the weight cubes it satisfies:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Replacing one weight block \\(s_j\\), with all other data fixed, changes \\(f\\) by at most \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Replacing one seed \\(c_e\\), with all other data fixed, changes \\(f\\) by at most \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Fix \\(k\\in[d]\\). Simultaneously replacing any seeds incident to \\(k\\), while keeping all nonincident seeds, all weights, and the question received at every opposite position \\(j\\ne k\\) from \\(\\{j,k\\}\\) unchanged, changes \\(f\\) by at most \\(2\\).</p><p>The set \\(A_*\\), function \\(F\\), and its formula are fixed from an independent set of the entire graph before the fresh seeds are drawn. A seed enters \\(f\\) only through its endpoint questions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take \\(A_*\\) to be the set of sum vectors of the stipulated high-norm vertices in the independent set. The number of those vertices gives the probability bound even if several vertices represent the same vector.</p><p>For distinct \\(x,y\\in A_*\\), their representing vertices are distinct and nonadjacent, so the edge definition gives\n\\[\n\\|x+y\\|_*&gt;2t.\n\\]\nIf \\(x=y\\), the same inequality follows from\n\\[\n\\|2x\\|_*=2\\|x\\|_*&gt;2t.\n\\]</p><p>Distance to a nonempty finite set is the minimum of the distances to its points. The triangle inequality makes each distance function \\(1\\)-Lipschitz; the half-difference defining \\(F\\) is therefore \\(1\\)-Lipschitz. Symmetry of the norm gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_*(-z,-A_*)=\\operatorname{dist}_*(z,A_*),\n\\]\nproving oddness.</p><p>For \\(z\\in A_*\\), its distance to \\(A_*\\) is zero, while\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_*(z,-A_*)\n=\n\\min_{x\\in A_*}\\|z+x\\|_*\n&gt;\n2t.\n\\]\nFiniteness preserves the strict inequality, so \\(F(z)&gt;t\\).</p><p>For fixed seeds, negating all weights preserves their joint law and negates \\(f\\). This proves\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf s}f(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf s)=0.\n\\]\nAlso \\(F(0)=0\\), and the increment bound from Proposition 1 implies \\(|f|\\le d\\) on the sampling space.</p><p>Round every continuous entry to the midpoint of its grid interval. The resulting entries are independent uniform elements of \\(T\\); interval-boundary choices have probability zero. The change in one increment has norm at most \\(1/p\\), by linearity in its weights and the increment bound. Thus rounding changes the argument of \\(F\\), and hence its value, by at most\n\\[\n\\frac d p&lt;\\frac t2.\n\\]</p><p>The unchanged seeds together with the rounded weights have exactly the uniform-vertex law. With probability at least \\(b_0\\), their sum belongs to \\(A_*\\), and on that event the unrounded value of \\(f\\) exceeds \\(t/2\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\mathbb Ef^2\n\\ge\n\\frac{b_0t^2}{4}\n=\n\\frac{b_0a^2d}{4}\n=\n\\nu d.\n\\]</p><p>Every increment on a weight cube has norm at most one. Replacing one weight block changes one increment by norm at most two, proving the first oscillation bound.</p><p>A single seed affects only its two endpoint queries; replacing it changes at most two increments, each by norm at most two, proving the second bound.</p><p>For the third bound, each position other than \\(k\\) keeps every question in its query, so only the increment at \\(k\\) may change. The same bound of two proves the claim.</p><p>All these arguments use the single fixed function \\(F\\); it is not chosen again for a sampled seed tuple.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a fixed \\(h\\)-element set \\(J\\) and fixed frozen data \\(\\mathbf a\\) such that, writing \\(H_j=H_j^{J,\\mathbf a}\\), \\( \\sum_{j\\in J} \\mathbb E_{u_j}\\operatorname{Var}_{s_j}H_j(u_j,s_j) \\ge \\frac{\\nu h}{4}. \\) …", "statement": "<p>Own-question variance under a restriction (paper Lemma 5.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">This proposition uses a generic product-space setting, rather than requiring that \\(f\\) arise from the graph.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d\\ge4\\) and \\(r,M\\ge1\\) be integers, and put\n\\[\n\\mathcal E=\\binom{[d]}2.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(e\\in\\mathcal E\\), independently draw \\(c_e\\) uniformly from \\([M]\\). At endpoint \\(j\\in e\\), this seed reveals a question \\(Q_{e,j}(c_e)\\), where \\(Q_{e,j}\\) is a fixed map to a finite set. No independence is assumed between the two questions from the same seed.</p><p class=\"li\">Independently of the seeds, draw \\(s_j\\) uniformly from \\([-1,1]^r\\), independently over \\(j\\in[d]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All initial expectations and \\(L^2\\)-norms refer to the product law on\n\\[\n\\Omega=[M]^{\\mathcal E}\\times([-1,1]^r)^d.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\) be a bounded Borel measurable function on \\(\\Omega\\). Let \\(\\nu&gt;0\\), and let \\(h\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le h\\le d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf s}f(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf s)=0\n\\quad\\text{for every }\\mathbf c,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb Ef^2\\ge\\nu d.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">With all unmentioned coordinates fixed, assume:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Changing one weight block \\(s_j\\) changes \\(f\\) by at most \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Changing one seed \\(c_e\\) changes \\(f\\) by at most \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. For each \\(k\\in[d]\\), simultaneously changing seeds incident to \\(k\\), while preserving \\(Q_{\\{k,j\\},j}(c_{\\{k,j\\}})\\) for every \\(j\\ne k\\), changes \\(f\\) by at most \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\n16h+32h^2\\le\\frac{\\nu d}{2}.\n\\]</p><p>Restrictions and conditional means:</p><p class=\"li\">Given \\(J\\subseteq[d]\\), its frozen data are\n\\[\n\\mathbf a=\n\\left(\n(c_e)_{e\\notin\\binom J2},\n(s_k)_{k\\notin J}\n\\right).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fixing these values leaves a function \\(f^*\\) of the hidden seeds \\((c_e)_{e\\in\\binom J2}\\) and weights \\((s_j)_{j\\in J}\\), with their original product law.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\in J\\), write\n\\[\nu_j=\n\\bigl(Q_{\\{j,k\\},j}(c_{\\{j,k\\}})\\bigr)_{k\\in J\\setminus\\{j\\}},\n\\]\nwith entries in increasing order of \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(H_j^{J,\\mathbf a}(u,s)\\) by inserting \\(s_j=s\\) into \\(f^*\\) and averaging all remaining variables conditional on \\(u_j=u\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Explicitly, each incident hidden seed is drawn independently and uniformly from its fiber under the relevant \\(Q_{\\{j,k\\},j}\\); other hidden seeds and other weights retain their original independent laws. On a question tuple of probability zero set \\(H_j^{J,\\mathbf a}=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">These averages are defined by finite averages and cube integrals with the frozen values plugged in, including when the frozen data have continuous coordinates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a fixed \\(h\\)-element set \\(J\\) and fixed frozen data \\(\\mathbf a\\) such that, writing \\(H_j=H_j^{J,\\mathbf a}\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\n\\mathbb E_{u_j}\\operatorname{Var}_{s_j}H_j(u_j,s_j)\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\nu h}{4}.\n\\]\nThe law in this inequality is the original product law of the remaining hidden seeds and batch weights. It is not conditioned on an event involving a draw of those remaining variables.</p>", "proof": "<p>The conditional mean \\(H_j\\) averages all unfrozen weights except \\(s_j\\). It also averages every hidden seed on a pair avoiding \\(j\\), and retains only \\(j\\)’s question on each hidden pair incident to \\(j\\). Lemmas 4.1–4.3 control the energy lost through these three types of averaging.</p><p>By Lemma 4.3,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n\\ge\n\\frac h d\n\\left(\n\\|f\\|_2^2-8dp_1-8d^2p_2\n\\right).\n\\]\nSince \\(d\\ge4\\),\n\\[\np_1\\le\\frac{2h}{d},\n\\qquad\np_2\\le\\frac{4h^2}{d^2}.\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\|f\\|_2^2\\ge\\nu d\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n&amp;\\ge\n\\frac h d\\left(\\nu d-16h-32h^2\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{\\nu h}{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere the last inequality is the assumed restriction condition.</p><p>The nonnegative measurable quantity \\(\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\\) is bounded, since \\(f\\) is bounded. Its mean implies that a set of choices \\((J,\\mathbf a)\\) of positive probability has\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\\ge\\frac{\\nu h}{4}.\n\\]\nChoose one such restriction.</p><p>The conditional averages were defined by explicit integration with its frozen coordinates plugged in. All hidden seeds and batch weights are then drawn with their original product law, establishing the claimed inequality and its asserted interpretation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The averaging operators are orthogonal projections.", "statement": "<p>Weight and seed decompositions and their energy budgets (the first two decomposition steps in the proof of paper Lemma 5.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the generic product space and function \\(f\\) from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W_j^0\\) average the weight block \\(s_j\\), retaining all other coordinates, and put\n\\[\nW_j^1=\\operatorname{Id}-W_j^0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_e^0\\) average the seed \\(c_e\\). For \\(j\\in e\\), let \\(O_{e,j}\\) average that seed conditional on \\(Q_{e,j}(c_e)\\), retaining all other coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S\\subseteq[d]\\), define\n\\[\n\\Pi_S=\n\\prod_{j\\in S}W_j^1\n\\prod_{j\\notin S}W_j^0,\n\\qquad\nf_S=\\Pi_Sf.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For every nonempty \\(S\\), fix a designated position \\(j(S)\\in S\\), for example \\(j(S)=\\min S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">With \\(j=j(S)\\), refine each seed coordinate by the following projections:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nj\\notin e:&amp;P_e^0,\\ \\operatorname{Id}-P_e^0,\\\\{}[2mm]\nj\\in e:&amp;P_e^0,\\ O_{e,j}-P_e^0,\\ \\operatorname{Id}-O_{e,j}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a choice \\(\\omega\\) of one displayed projection at every seed, let \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\) be their product applied to \\(f_S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T_{S,\\omega}\\subseteq\\mathcal E\\) be the seeds at which the chosen projection is not \\(P_e^0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N_{S,\\omega}\\subseteq[d]\\setminus\\{j(S)\\}\\) be the positions \\(k\\) for which the projection at \\(\\{j(S),k\\}\\) is \\(\\operatorname{Id}-O_{\\{j(S),k\\},j(S)}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The averaging operators are orthogonal projections. Operators on distinct coordinates commute, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ran}(P_e^0)\\subseteq\\operatorname{ran}(O_{e,j}),\n\\qquad\nP_e^0O_{e,j}=O_{e,j}P_e^0=P_e^0.\n\\]\nThe two endpoint projections on a single seed are not assumed to commute.</p><p>The functions \\(f_S\\) form an orthogonal decomposition with \\(f_\\varnothing=0\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_{S\\ne\\varnothing}|S|\\|f_S\\|_2^2\\le4d.\n\\]\nThe functions \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\), over nonempty \\(S\\) and their allowed choices \\(\\omega\\), form an orthogonal refinement satisfying\n\\[\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2=\\|f\\|_2^2\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}|T_{S,\\omega}|\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2\n=\n\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal E}\\|f-P_e^0f\\|_2^2\n\\le16\\binom d2\n\\le8d^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Each averaging is an orthogonal projection in \\(L^2(\\Omega)\\): averaging twice gives the same function, and integrating over the retained data shows self-adjointness. The displayed nesting and projection identities follow from the fact that averaging a seed completely averages, in particular, its question. Operators on distinct coordinates commute by the product law and Fubini’s theorem. The two endpoint projections \\(O_{e,j},O_{e,k}\\) on a single seed need not commute; no such commutation is used.</p><p>Expanding\n\\[\n\\prod_j(W_j^0+W_j^1)\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nf=\\sum_Sf_S.\n\\]\nDistinct \\(S\\)’s give orthogonal components, because their projections have a factor \\(W_j^0W_j^1=0\\) at some position. The assumption \\(\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf s}f(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf s)=0\\) says that \\(f_\\varnothing=0\\).</p><p>Furthermore,\n\\[\nf-W_j^0f=\\sum_{S\\ni j}f_S,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\sum_{S\\ne\\varnothing}|S|\\|f_S\\|_2^2\n=\n\\sum_{j=1}^d\\|f-W_j^0f\\|_2^2\n\\le4d.\n\\]\nIndeed, on every fiber obtained by fixing the other coordinates, the one-weight oscillation bound implies \\(|f-W_j^0f|\\le2\\).</p><p>For fixed nonempty \\(S\\), the nested ranges make the three incident-seed projections pairwise orthogonal, and their sum is the identity. Thus the \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\) give an orthogonal decomposition of \\(f_S\\). The seed projections commute with all weight projections, so every \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\) remains in \\(\\operatorname{ran}(\\Pi_S)\\). Consequently the components for different \\(S\\)’s are also orthogonal, even though the endpoint used to refine a seed can change with \\(S\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2=\\|f\\|_2^2.\n\\]</p><p>For each fixed \\(e\\), applying \\(\\operatorname{Id}-P_e^0\\) keeps exactly the components with \\(e\\in T_{S,\\omega}\\). Orthogonality and the one-seed oscillation bound give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}|T_{S,\\omega}|\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal E}\\|f-P_e^0f\\|_2^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le16\\binom d2\n\\le8d^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(|f-P_e^0f|\\le4\\) follows by averaging values whose oscillation is at most \\(4\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each \\(D_k\\) is an orthogonal projection retaining all weights, all nonincident seeds, and the opposite endpoint question at each seed incident to \\(k\\).", "statement": "<p>Charging invisible incident information to stars (the star estimate in the proof of paper Lemma 5.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the product space, function \\(f\\), and star oscillation assumption from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the orthogonal weight components \\(f_S\\), designated positions \\(j(S)\\), and refined components \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Thus \\(N_{S,\\omega}\\) consists of positions \\(k\\ne j(S)\\) where the seed projection on \\(\\{j(S),k\\}\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Id}-O_{\\{j(S),k\\},j(S)}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(k\\in[d]\\), define\n\\[\nD_k=\\prod_{j\\ne k}O_{\\{k,j\\},j}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each \\(D_k\\) is an orthogonal projection retaining all weights, all nonincident seeds, and the opposite endpoint question at each seed incident to \\(k\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\|f-D_kf\\|_2^2\\le4,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}|N_{S,\\omega}|\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2\\le4d.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The factors defining \\(D_k\\) act on distinct seed coordinates, so \\(D_k\\) is an orthogonal projection. More explicitly, it retains all weights, all seeds not incident to \\(k\\), and the opposite endpoint question at each incident seed. Given these data, the incident seeds have independent conditional laws on their respective fibers; their product gives exactly the displayed operator.</p><p>The star oscillation hypothesis bounds the oscillation of \\(f\\) on each such fiber by \\(2\\). It follows that\n\\[\n\\|f-D_kf\\|_2^2\\le4.\n\\]</p><p>For a component designated at \\(j\\), charge invisible information on \\(\\{j,k\\}\\) to the star centered at \\(k\\): that star retains \\(j\\)’s question on this pair. For every \\(j\\ne k\\), the range of \\(D_k\\) is contained in that of \\(O_{\\{k,j\\},j}\\). Distance to the larger closed subspace is no greater than distance to the smaller one. Thus, for every \\(v\\in L^2\\),\n\\[\n\\|(\\operatorname{Id}-O_{\\{k,j\\},j})v\\|_2^2\n\\le\n\\|(\\operatorname{Id}-D_k)v\\|_2^2.\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(S\\) and \\(k\\ne j(S)\\), the seed decomposition shows that\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{Id}-O_{\\{k,j(S)\\},j(S)})f_S\n\\]\nis the sum of exactly the components with \\(k\\in N_{S,\\omega}\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}|N_{S,\\omega}|\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{S\\ne\\varnothing}\n\\sum_{k\\ne j(S)}\n\\|(\\operatorname{Id}-O_{\\{k,j(S)\\},j(S)})f_S\\|_2^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_{k=1}^d\\sum_{S\\ne\\varnothing}\n\\|(\\operatorname{Id}-D_k)f_S\\|_2^2\\\\\n&amp;=\n\\sum_{k=1}^d\\|(\\operatorname{Id}-D_k)f\\|_2^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le4d.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe inequality applies the preceding projection bound and adds the nonnegative terms with \\(j(S)=k\\). The next equality uses the fact that \\(D_k\\) commutes with the weight projections, so the images of distinct \\(f_S\\)’s remain orthogonal.</p><p>This argument uses neither products of different \\(D_k\\)’s nor commutation of opposite endpoint projections on one seed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(J\\), \\( \\mathbb E_{\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a) = \\sum_{j\\in J}\\|K_{J,j}f\\|_2^2. \\) A component \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\), counted only in its designated term \\(j=j(S)\\), is preserved whenever …", "statement": "<p>What a restriction keeps and its average retained energy (the restriction-survival calculation in the proof of paper Lemma 5.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit all assumptions of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the averaging operators of Lemma 4.1 and the orthogonal components \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\), with designated position \\(j(S)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each component, \\(T_{S,\\omega}\\) is the set of seeds assigned a nonconstant projection, and \\(N_{S,\\omega}\\) is the set of positions whose incident projection at the designated position is invisible to that position’s own question.</p><p class=\"li\">For fixed \\(J\\subseteq[d]\\) and \\(j\\in J\\), define\n\\[\nK_{J,j}\n=\nW_j^1\n\\prod_{k\\in J\\setminus\\{j\\}}W_k^0\n\\prod_{e\\in\\binom{J\\setminus\\{j\\}}2}P_e^0\n\\prod_{k\\in J\\setminus\\{j\\}}O_{\\{j,k\\},j}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">With the frozen data and conditional means of Proposition 4, put\n\\[\n\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n=\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\n\\mathbb E_{u_j}\\operatorname{Var}_{s_j}\nH_j^{J,\\mathbf a}(u_j,s_j).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">When \\(J\\) is uniform among the \\(h\\)-element subsets of \\([d]\\), put\n\\[\np_1=\\frac{h-1}{d-1},\n\\qquad\np_2=\\frac{(h-1)(h-2)}{(d-1)(d-2)}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For fixed \\(J\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n=\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\\|K_{J,j}f\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nA component \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\), counted only in its designated term \\(j=j(S)\\), is preserved whenever\n\\[\nS\\cap J=\\{j\\},\n\\qquad\nN_{S,\\omega}\\cap J=\\varnothing,\n\\qquad\nT_{S,\\omega}\\cap\\binom{J\\setminus\\{j\\}}2=\\varnothing.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n\\ge\n\\frac h d\n\\left(\n\\|f\\|_2^2-8dp_1-8d^2p_2\n\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>All seed factors in \\(K_{J,j}\\) act on distinct coordinates. Before applying \\(W_j^1\\), these operators average exactly the unfrozen data other than the own questions \\(u_j\\) and own weights \\(s_j\\). They retain the frozen data \\(\\mathbf a\\) as variables. The final centering \\(W_j^1\\) subtracts the mean in \\(s_j\\), which is independent of the retained seed information. Thus\n\\[\nK_{J,j}f\n=\nH_j^{J,\\mathbf a}(u_j,s_j)\n-\n\\mathbb E_{s_j'}H_j^{J,\\mathbf a}(u_j,s_j')\n\\]\nas functions on the original product space. Integration over the initially random frozen data therefore gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n=\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\\|K_{J,j}f\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThis identity uses only independent seed coordinates. Conditioning on the own questions restricts each incident hidden seed to its own fiber.</p><p>Consider \\(f_{S,\\omega}\\) and \\(j=j(S)\\). First note that \\(K_{J,j}\\) commutes with every \\(\\Pi_S\\). Thus its outputs from different weight supports are orthogonal, and\n\\[\n\\|K_{J,j}f\\|_2^2\n=\n\\sum_S\\|K_{J,j}f_S\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThe weight factors in \\(K_{J,j}\\) keep \\(f_S\\) precisely when \\(S\\cap J=\\{j\\}\\).</p><p>For those \\(S\\) whose designated position is \\(j\\), the seed factors are diagonal in the chosen seed decomposition: \\(P_e^0\\) on a nonincident hidden seed keeps just its constant component, and \\(O_{e,j}\\) on an incident hidden seed keeps its constant and own-question components. The other seed coordinates are untouched. They therefore keep exactly the components satisfying the last two preservation conditions, with no cancellation among them.</p><p>Discarding all other nonnegative squared norms and counting each component solely in its designated term gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n\\ge\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\text{the three preservation conditions hold}\\}}\n\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(J\\) uniformly among all \\(h\\)-element subsets of \\([d]\\). For a fixed component, its designated position belongs to \\(J\\) with probability \\(h/d\\). Conditional on this inclusion, a specified other position belongs to \\(J\\) with probability \\(p_1\\), and a specified pair avoiding the designated position lies in \\(J\\) with probability \\(p_2\\).</p><p>The first two preservation conditions fail only if one of\n\\[\nS\\setminus\\{j(S)\\}\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nN_{S,\\omega}\n\\]\nis included. The third can fail only at a pair in \\(T_{S,\\omega}\\) avoiding \\(j(S)\\). A union bound therefore gives conditional survival probability at least\n\\[\n1-p_1\\bigl(|S|-1+|N_{S,\\omega}|\\bigr)-p_2|T_{S,\\omega}|.\n\\]\nCharging all of \\(T_{S,\\omega}\\) only decreases this lower bound. The bound remains valid when the displayed expression is negative.</p><p>By Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2 and the orthogonal refinement of each \\(f_S\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{S,\\omega}\n\\bigl(|S|-1+|N_{S,\\omega}|\\bigr)\n\\|f_{S,\\omega}\\|_2^2\n\\le8d.\n\\]\nCombining the preservation inequality, the survival bound, and the energy identities of Lemma 4.1 yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{J,\\mathbf a}\\mathcal V(J,\\mathbf a)\n\\ge\n\\frac h d\n\\left(\n\\|f\\|_2^2-8dp_1-8d^2p_2\n\\right).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist deterministic total measurable maps \\( \\mathcal L_j(u,s)\\subseteq\\Lambda_{i_j(u)} \\) returning at most \\(\\ell\\) valid labels, with the empty-list fallback on unsupported tuples, such that each map uses only …", "statement": "<p>Local lists and compatible hits (paper Proposition 6.3, using paper Lemmas 6.1–6.2).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph construction, queries, labels, slots, compatible selections \\(\\mathcal I\\), increments, and norm of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the finite set \\(A_*\\) and the function\n\\[\nF(z)=\\frac12\\left(\n\\operatorname{dist}_*(z,-A_*)-\\operatorname{dist}_*(z,A_*)\n\\right)\n\\]\nsupplied by Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\nu=\\frac1{2^7m^3},\n\\qquad\n\\beta=\\frac{\\nu}{8},\n\\qquad\n\\ell=\\frac2\\beta,\n\\qquad\nh=2\\ell.\n\\]\nBoth \\(\\ell,h\\) are positive integers depending only on \\(m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\nh&lt;d,\\qquad\n16h+32h^2\\le\\frac{\\nu d}{2}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(J\\subseteq[d]\\), \\(|J|=h\\), and frozen data \\(\\mathbf a\\) as supplied by Proposition 4 for\n\\[\nf(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf s)\n=\nF\\!\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^dz(i_j(\\mathbf c),s_j)\\right).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathbf a\\) fixes every seed outside \\(\\mathcal E_J=\\binom J2\\) and every weight outside \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(f^*(\\mathbf c_J,\\mathbf s_J)\\) for the resulting restricted function. The remaining law consists of independent uniform hidden seeds \\(c_e\\in[M]\\), \\(e\\in\\mathcal E_J\\), and independent uniform weight blocks \\(s_j\\in[-1,1]^r\\), \\(j\\in J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\in J\\), let\n\\[\nu_j=(q_{jk})_{k\\in J\\setminus\\{j\\}},\n\\]\nordered by increasing \\(k\\). Together with \\(\\mathbf a\\), it determines the completed query \\(i_j(u_j)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\kappa_j(u)\\) be the conditional distribution of all hidden seeds given \\(u_j=u\\). Each incident seed is uniform on the fiber producing the specified question at \\(j\\); each nonincident hidden seed is uniform on \\([M]\\). These conditional seed coordinates are independent.</p><p class=\"li\">For supported \\(u\\), define\n\\[\nH_j(u,s)\n=\n\\mathbb E_{\\substack{\\mathbf c_J\\sim\\kappa_j(u),\\\\\n(s_k)_{k\\in J\\setminus\\{j\\}}}}\n\\left.f^*(\\mathbf c_J,\\mathbf s_J)\\right|_{s_j=s}.\n\\]\nThe other weight blocks have their original independent uniform laws. Set \\(H_j=0\\) on unsupported own-question tuples.</p><p class=\"li\">The fixed restriction satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\n\\mathbb E_{u_j}\\operatorname{Var}_{s_j}H_j(u_j,s_j)\n\\ge\\frac{\\nu h}{4}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For any realized lists \\(L_j\\subseteq\\Lambda_{i_j}\\), define\n\\[\nZ=\n\\max_{I\\in\\mathcal I}\n\\#\\{j\\in J:\\exists\\lambda\\in L_j\\text{ with }(i_j,\\lambda)\\in I\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist deterministic total measurable maps\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j(u,s)\\subseteq\\Lambda_{i_j(u)}\n\\]\nreturning at most \\(\\ell\\) valid labels, with the empty-list fallback on unsupported tuples, such that each map uses only its own questions \\(u\\) and weights \\(s\\), once \\(\\Phi,F,J,\\mathbf a\\) and the conditional kernels are fixed.</p><p>For the actual lists \\(L_j=\\mathcal L_j(u_j,s_j)\\), under the fixed restricted product law,\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ\\ge\\frac{\\beta h}{2}=2.\n\\]\nThe maps have no additional access to actual hidden seed occurrences or other actual batch inputs. Their efficient computability is not required.</p>", "proof": "<p>For supported \\(u\\), define the slot-ordered list\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j(u,s)\n=\n\\left\\{\n\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_{i_j(u)}:\n\\left|\n\\bar g_{j,\\operatorname{slot}_{i_j(u)}(\\lambda)}(u,s)\n\\right|\n\\ge\\frac\\beta2\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor an unsupported own-question tuple, let the list be empty. At an actual draw write\n\\[\nL_j=\\mathcal L_j(u_j,s_j).\n\\]</p><p>The mean block bound of Lemma 5.2 gives\n\\[\n\\frac\\beta2|\\mathcal L_j(u,s)|\n\\le\n\\|\\bar g_j(u,s)\\|_1\n\\le1,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_j(u,s)|\\le\\frac2\\beta=\\ell.\n\\]\nIt also gives zero dummy entries, so thresholding returns only valid labels. Finite threshold comparisons of measurable functions, with an empty-list fallback, prove totality and measurability.</p><p>We first transfer the retained mass of the own conditional means to the full gradient rule \\(g\\), and then apply its pointwise hull bound.</p><p>For an actual own input, let \\(T_j\\subseteq[r]\\) be the set of slots of \\(L_j\\), and write \\(\\mathcal G_j\\) for the information \\((u_j,s_j)\\). The conditional mean \\(\\bar g_j\\) is a version of\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[g_j\\mid\\mathcal G_j],\n\\]\ndefined by the displayed kernel even at own values of probability zero.</p><p>For slots outside \\(T_j\\), the conditional block bound and threshold give\n\\[\n\\sum_{t'\\notin T_j}|\\bar g_{j,t'}|^2\n\\le\n\\frac\\beta2\\sum_{t'\\notin T_j}|\\bar g_{j,t'}|\n\\le\n\\frac\\beta2.\n\\]\nFor list slots, each mean entry has magnitude at most one, and conditional Jensen gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{t'\\in T_j}|\\bar g_{j,t'}|^2\n\\le\n\\sum_{t'\\in T_j}|\\bar g_{j,t'}|\n\\le\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n\\left.\n\\sum_{t'\\in T_j}|g_{j,t'}|\n\\right|\\mathcal G_j\n\\right].\n\\]\nThe last inequality uses that \\(T_j\\) is determined by \\(\\mathcal G_j\\).</p><p>Taking expectations, summing, and applying the energy bound of Lemma 5.2 yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\\sum_{\\lambda\\in L_j}\n|g_{j,\\operatorname{slot}_{i_j}(\\lambda)}|\n\\ge\n\\beta h-\\frac\\beta2h\n=\n\\frac{\\beta h}{2}.\n\\]</p><p>For arbitrary fixed queries and arbitrary lists of valid labels, the sum of absolute values over their slot sets is a convex function of the full vector in \\(\\mathbb R^{J\\times[r]}\\). On a signed generator \\(\\pm v_I(\\mathbf i)\\), it equals the number of positions hit by that selection \\(I\\), since each block contains at most one selected slot. Its value on every point of the hull in Lemma 5.1 is consequently at most the maximum such hit count.</p><p>At every realized tuple, apply this deterministic statement to the realized lists and the full vector \\(g\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\\sum_{\\lambda\\in L_j}\n|g_{j,\\operatorname{slot}_{i_j}(\\lambda)}|\n\\le Z.\n\\]\nNo independence between the lists and the gradient, or between positions, is required. Combining the last two inequalities proves\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ\\ge\\frac{\\beta h}{2}=2,\n\\]\nusing \\(\\ell=2/\\beta\\) and \\(h=2\\ell\\).</p><p>Finally, the formulas for \\(H_j\\) and \\(\\bar g_j\\) integrate over hidden seeds against the fixed law \\(\\kappa_j(u)\\), and over other weights against their fixed cube laws. Their actual realized values are not inputs to the resulting mean function. Thus the list has exactly the stated own-input dependence. No efficiency of this rule is needed.</p><p>The kernels and list maps remain fixed throughout the subsequent argument. Conditioning later on other hidden seeds or on all weights changes the distribution of the inputs to these maps; it does not recompute their conditional averages. Locality, validity, and list-size bounds continue to hold for every fixed weight choice. The lower bound on \\(\\mathbb EZ\\) concerns the original restricted product law.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a deterministic measurable rule \\( g=(g_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\mathbb R^{J\\times[r]} \\) defined at every batch-query and real-weight tuple, that equals \\(\\nabla\\psi_{\\mathbf i}\\) outside a finite union of proper affine …", "statement": "<p>A gradient rule in the compatibility hull (paper Lemma 6.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the fixed source instance, \\(A_*\\), function \\(F\\), batch \\(J\\), and frozen data from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">The increments outside \\(J\\) are fixed, because their queries use no seed in \\(\\mathcal E_J\\). Put\n\\[\nc_0=\\sum_{k\\notin J}z(i_k,s_k).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a fixed tuple of batch queries \\(\\mathbf i=(i_j)_{j\\in J}\\), define on the whole real weight space\n\\[\n\\psi_{\\mathbf i}(\\mathbf s_J)\n=\nF\\!\\left(c_0+\\sum_{j\\in J}z(i_j,s_j)\\right).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(I\\in\\mathcal I\\), let \\(v_I(\\mathbf i)\\in\\mathbb R^{J\\times[r]}\\) have entry one at \\((j,\\operatorname{slot}_{i_j}(\\lambda))\\) when \\((i_j,\\lambda)\\in I\\), and zero elsewhere. Each block has at most one nonzero entry.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a deterministic measurable rule\n\\[\ng=(g_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\mathbb R^{J\\times[r]}\n\\]\ndefined at every batch-query and real-weight tuple, that equals \\(\\nabla\\psi_{\\mathbf i}\\) outside a finite union of proper affine hyperplanes for each fixed query tuple and fixed frozen data.</p><p>At every tuple,\n\\[\ng\\in\\operatorname{conv}\\{\\pm v_I(\\mathbf i):I\\in\\mathcal I\\}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\|g_j\\|_1\\le1,\n\\qquad\ng_{j,t'}=0\n\\quad\\text{if }t'\\notin\\operatorname{slot}_{i_j}(\\Lambda_{i_j}).\n\\]\nThe rule uses the fixed formula for \\(F\\), frozen increments, and supplied batch queries and weights. It does not inspect which hidden seed occurrences produced those queries.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathbf1_I\\in\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal P}\\) be the indicator vector of \\(I\\). For\n\\[\n\\zeta\\in A_*\\cup(-A_*),\\qquad I\\in\\mathcal I,\\qquad\n\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\},\n\\]\nform the finite collection of affine expressions\n\\[\nL_{\\zeta,I,\\varepsilon}(\\mathbf s_J)\n=\n\\varepsilon\\langle\\mathbf1_I,c_0-\\zeta\\rangle\n+\n\\varepsilon\\langle v_I(\\mathbf i),\\mathbf s_J\\rangle.\n\\]\nFor either \\(C=A_*\\) or \\(C=-A_*\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_*\\!\\left(\nc_0+\\sum_{j\\in J}z(i_j,s_j),C\n\\right)\n=\n\\min_{\\zeta\\in C}\n\\max_{\\substack{I\\in\\mathcal I,\\\\\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}}}\nL_{\\zeta,I,\\varepsilon}(\\mathbf s_J).\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\psi_{\\mathbf i}\\) is continuous and piecewise affine, with finitely many pieces.</p><p>Compare all pairs of candidates. If two candidates with different slope vectors in the unfrozen weights tie in value, set \\(g=0\\). Otherwise take the true gradient of the finite formula for \\(\\psi_{\\mathbf i}\\). This latter choice is well-defined.</p><p>Two candidates with the same slope either never tie or are identical affine functions. All other comparisons are strict at such a point and persist in a neighborhood; each minimum and maximum there selects one affine function up to identical copies. The resulting formula is affine in that neighborhood. Fixing a deterministic order on candidates resolves any choice between identical copies without changing its gradient.</p><p>Each discarded equality has a nonzero normal in the unfrozen coordinates, hence defines a proper affine hyperplane. No choice of frozen weights changes that fact, since frozen weights change the offsets but not the slopes. Identical slopes caused by repeated endpoint questions or other coincidences are allowed.</p><p>Finite comparisons prove measurability, and the rule is defined even at query tuples that cannot arise from the hidden seed law. Its formula uses the queries themselves and no additional seed information.</p><p>At a point not discarded, choose an active affine expression in each distance formula. Write their slopes as\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_-v_{I_-}(\\mathbf i)\n\\quad\\text{for the distance to }-A_*,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_+v_{I_+}(\\mathbf i)\n\\quad\\text{for the distance to }A_*.\n\\]\nSince \\(F\\) is half the difference of these distances,\n\\[\ng=\n\\frac12\\left(\n\\varepsilon_-v_{I_-}(\\mathbf i)\n-\n\\varepsilon_+v_{I_+}(\\mathbf i)\n\\right).\n\\]\nBoth vectors in this average are signed compatible-incidence vectors, proving the hull assertion. At discarded points, the chosen value zero also belongs to the symmetric hull.</p><p>Every signed incidence vector has block \\(\\ell_1\\)-norm at most one and zero entries in unused slots, so convexity proves the two block assertions at every tuple.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At every own input, \\( \\|\\bar g_j(u,s)\\|_1\\le1, \\) and \\(\\bar g_{j,t'}(u,s)=0\\) on unused slots of \\(i_j(u)\\).", "statement": "<p>Own-question conditional gradients and cube variance (paper Lemma 6.2, together with the preceding conditional-gradient definition).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the fixed restricted law, kernels \\(\\kappa_j\\), conditional means \\(H_j\\), and parameters \\(\\nu,\\beta,h\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\) be the rule of Lemma 5.1. A hidden seed tuple is used in evaluating \\(g\\) only to form the supplied queries.</p><p class=\"li\">For supported \\(u\\) and any \\(s\\in\\mathbb R^r\\), define\n\\[\n\\bar g_j(u,s)\n=\n\\mathbb E_{\\substack{\\mathbf c_J\\sim\\kappa_j(u),\\\\\n(s_k)_{k\\in J\\setminus\\{j\\}}}}\n\\left.g_j(\\mathbf c_J,\\mathbf s_J)\\right|_{s_j=s}.\n\\]\nSet \\(\\bar g_j(u,s)=0\\) for unsupported own-question tuples.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every own input,\n\\[\n\\|\\bar g_j(u,s)\\|_1\\le1,\n\\]\nand \\(\\bar g_{j,t'}(u,s)=0\\) on unused slots of \\(i_j(u)\\).</p><p>For every supported \\(u\\), the function \\(H_j(u,\\cdot)\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz with respect to \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty\\). For almost every \\(s\\in[-1,1]^r\\),\n\\[\n\\partial_{s^{(t')}}H_j(u,s)=\\bar g_{j,t'}(u,s)\n\\qquad(t'\\in[r]).\n\\]\nIts variance satisfies the dimension-independent bound\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}_sH_j(u,s)\n\\le\n2\\mathbb E_s\\|\\bar g_j(u,s)\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\n\\mathbb E\\|\\bar g_j(u_j,s_j)\\|_2^2\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\nu h}{8}\n=\n\\beta h.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For supported \\(u\\), its query \\(i_j(u)\\) is fixed throughout the conditional integral. Convexity and the block bounds in Lemma 5.1 give, at every own input,\n\\[\n\\|\\bar g_j(u,s)\\|_1\\le1,\n\\]\nand zero entries on unused slots.</p><p>Replacing the own block \\(s\\) by \\(s'\\), with a seed configuration and other weights fixed, changes the integrand defining \\(H_j\\) by at most \\(\\|s-s'\\|_\\infty\\). The same bound holds after averaging. In particular, every scalar-coordinate difference quotient of the integrand is bounded in absolute value by one.</p><p>For each fixed hidden seed tuple, the full gradient identification in Lemma 5.1 holds outside a null set in all batch weights. Fubini implies that for almost every own \\(s\\), it holds for almost all other weight blocks. The conditional seed law \\(\\kappa_j(u)\\) has finite support and does not depend on \\(s\\), so the exceptional sets may be united over its support and over the finitely many weight coordinates.</p><p>At the remaining own values, the coordinate difference quotients converge almost surely to \\(g_{j,t'}\\). Dominated convergence gives\n\\[\n\\partial_{s^{(t')}}H_j(u,s)=\\bar g_{j,t'}(u,s);\n\\]\nthe boundary of the cube has measure zero.</p><p>Every coordinate-line restriction of \\(H_j(u,\\cdot)\\) is Lipschitz and hence absolutely continuous. By Fubini applied to the derivative identity, for almost every fixing of the other own coordinates, its derivative agrees almost everywhere with the corresponding entry of \\(\\bar g_j\\). The fundamental theorem of calculus therefore expresses differences between any two values on such a line as the integral of that entry.</p><p>Let \\(w_0\\) be the restriction of \\(H_j(u,\\cdot)\\) to one of these lines. For \\(x,y\\in[-1,1]\\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|w_0(x)-w_0(y)|^2\n&amp;\\le\n|x-y|\n\\int_{\\min(x,y)}^{\\max(x,y)}|w_0'(z)|^2\\,dz\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n2\\int_{-1}^1|w_0'(z)|^2\\,dz.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor independent uniform \\(X,Y\\in[-1,1]\\), this implies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var}(w_0(X))\n&amp;=\n\\frac12\\mathbb E|w_0(X)-w_0(Y)|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\int_{-1}^1|w_0'(z)|^2\\,dz\\\\\n&amp;=\n2\\mathbb E|w_0'(X)|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>The product decomposition of Efron and Stein bounds variance by the sum of the averaged one-coordinate conditional variances. To see this consequence directly, decompose the identity at each scalar coordinate into its averaging projection and its orthogonal complement. The resulting orthogonal components are indexed by subsets of coordinates. Total variance counts every nonconstant squared component once, while the sum of one-coordinate conditional variances counts it once for each member of its nonempty index subset.</p><p>Applying the line estimate, integrating over the other coordinates, and summing proves\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}_sH_j(u,s)\n\\le\n2\\mathbb E_s\\|\\bar g_j(u,s)\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nAveraging over the own tuples and summing over \\(j\\), then using the retained-variance assumption in Proposition 5, gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in J}\n\\mathbb E\\|\\bar g_j(u_j,s_j)\\|_2^2\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\nu h}{8}\n=\n\\beta h.\n\\]</p><p>A particular own weight value can make an entire slice of the other weights exceptional. The derivative identity is only an almost-everywhere assertion, and the final energy bound is integrated over the restricted law. In contrast, the hull and block bounds of Lemma 5.1, and the conditional block bounds above, hold at every input, including exceptional weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(j<k\\) in \\(J\\), \\( \\Pr(E_{jk})\\le\\ell^2\\sigma. \\) Consequently, \\( \\mathbb EZ \\le 1+\\binom h2\\ell^2\\sigma = \\frac32-\\frac1{2h} < \\frac32. \\)", "statement": "<p>Decoding a pair (paper Lemma 7.1).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Phi\\) be a Label Cover instance, and use the queries, local labels, slots, and compatible selections \\(\\mathcal I\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J\\subseteq[d]\\) have cardinality \\(h\\ge2\\), and let \\(\\ell\\ge1\\) be an integer. Put\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\frac1{h^2\\ell^2},\n\\]\nand assume \\(\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)\\le\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix frozen data \\(\\mathbf a\\) consisting of every seed outside \\(\\binom J2\\) and every weight outside \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The remaining seeds \\(c_e\\), \\(e\\in\\binom J2\\), are independent uniform elements of \\([M]\\). The remaining weight blocks \\(s_j\\), \\(j\\in J\\), are independent uniforms on \\([-1,1]^r\\), independent of the seeds.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u_j\\) be position \\(j\\)’s tuple of endpoint questions from its incident hidden seeds. Together with \\(\\mathbf a\\), it determines the query \\(i_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider fixed total measurable maps\n\\[\nL_j=\\mathcal L_j(u_j,s_j)\\subseteq\\Lambda_{i_j},\n\\qquad |L_j|\\le\\ell.\n\\]\nEach map receives only the indicated own questions and weights, and returns the empty list on an unsupported own-question tuple. Order each list by its slots.</p><p class=\"li\">For distinct \\(j,k\\in J\\), let \\(E_{jk}\\) be the event that some \\(I\\in\\mathcal I\\) contains \\((i_j,\\lambda_j)\\) and \\((i_k,\\lambda_k)\\) for labels \\(\\lambda_j\\in L_j\\), \\(\\lambda_k\\in L_k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nZ=\n\\max_{I\\in\\mathcal I}\n\\#\\{j\\in J:\\text{some }\\lambda\\in L_j\n\\text{ has }(i_j,\\lambda)\\in I\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(j&lt;k\\) in \\(J\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(E_{jk})\\le\\ell^2\\sigma.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ\n\\le\n1+\\binom h2\\ell^2\\sigma\n=\n\\frac32-\\frac1{2h}\n&lt;\n\\frac32.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(j&lt;k\\) and write \\(e=\\{j,k\\}\\). Fix all batch weights and all hidden seeds except \\(c_e\\). By independence, the remaining occurrence \\(c_e\\) is still uniform on \\([M]\\).</p><p>Position \\(j\\)’s only varying question is \\(x_{c_e}\\in U\\), and position \\(k\\)’s only varying question is \\(y_{c_e}\\in V\\). Every other argument of each list map is now a constant, while the maps themselves remain fixed. For the maps constructed in Proposition 5, their conditional averages are not recomputed: the actual values just fixed are not supplied as new arguments to those averages.</p><p>For \\(x\\in U\\), form the tuple \\(u_j^x\\) by putting \\(x\\) in the distinguished \\(e\\)-coordinate of \\(u_j\\) and retaining all its other, now fixed, coordinates. Define \\(u_k^y\\) for \\(y\\in V\\) in the same way.</p><p>Choose default symbols \\(a_U\\in\\Sigma_U\\) and \\(a_V\\in\\Sigma_V\\). For each pair of ranks \\(\\rho,\\tau\\in[\\ell]\\), define a labeling \\(A_{\\rho,\\tau}\\) of the entire source instance as follows:</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(x\\in U\\), evaluate \\(\\mathcal L_j(u_j^x,s_j)\\). If rank \\(\\rho\\) is present, give \\(x\\) its value in that label; otherwise give it \\(a_U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(y\\in V\\), use its value in the label at rank \\(\\tau\\) of \\(\\mathcal L_k(u_k^y,s_k)\\), or \\(a_V\\) if the rank is absent.</p><p>This gives one symbol at every source variable. If a substituted tuple has probability zero, its empty list invokes the same fallback. If the variable already occurs in another coordinate of the query, its value in a present label is still unique: a local label is an assignment on the set of distinct variables in the scope. Thus repeated variables, empty valid-label sets, and missing ranks cause no ambiguity.</p><p>The assigned symbol on \\(U\\) depends only on \\(x\\), and the assigned symbol on \\(V\\) only on \\(y\\), besides fixed data. It does not use the occurrence index or its projection map. The source promise consequently gives\n\\[\n\\Pr_{c\\text{ uniform in }[M]}\n\\left[\n\\pi_c(A_{\\rho,\\tau}(x_c))=A_{\\rho,\\tau}(y_c)\n\\right]\n\\le\\sigma.\n\\]</p><p>For every actual remaining occurrence \\(c_e\\), the reconstructed tuples\n\\[\nu_j^{x_{c_e}},\\qquad u_k^{y_{c_e}}\n\\]\nare supported under the original hidden-seed law: the full seed tuple comprising the fixed other seeds and this occurrence has positive probability in that product law. They are exactly the own tuples at which the actual lists are evaluated.</p><p>Suppose \\(E_{jk}\\) occurs and choose the ranks \\(\\rho,\\tau\\) of labels witnessing it. The endpoint \\(x_{c_e}\\) belongs to \\(P(i_j)\\), and \\(y_{c_e}\\) to \\(P(i_k)\\). Compatibility requires the induced labeling to satisfy every source constraint with both endpoints in the union of the selected scopes. In particular it satisfies the sampled occurrence \\(c_e\\).</p><p>Its endpoint labels are exactly those used by \\(A_{\\rho,\\tau}\\), so\n\\[\n\\pi_{c_e}(A_{\\rho,\\tau}(x_{c_e}))\n=\nA_{\\rho,\\tau}(y_{c_e}).\n\\]\nThis uses the sampled occurrence even when other occurrences have the same endpoints.</p><p>Union bounding the source-soundness inequality over the \\(\\ell^2\\) rank pairs proves the pair estimate for the fixed weights and other seeds. It is uniform in those fixed values, so averaging proves\n\\[\n\\Pr(E_{jk})\\le\\ell^2\\sigma.\n\\]\nNo conditioning on compatible labels or successful ranks is used.</p><p>For every integer \\(z\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nz\\le1+\\binom z2,\n\\]\nsince the difference is \\((z-1)(z-2)/2\\ge0\\). Choose a selection attaining the maximum defining \\(Z\\). Each pair among its \\(Z\\) hit positions witnesses the corresponding event \\(E_{jk}\\). Consequently, pointwise,\n\\[\nZ\n\\le\n1+\\binom Z2\n\\le\n1+\\sum_{\\{j,k\\}\\in\\binom J2}\\mathbf1_{E_{jk}}.\n\\]\nTaking expectations, using the pair estimate, and substituting \\(\\sigma=1/(h^2\\ell^2)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ\n\\le\n1+\\binom h2\\ell^2\\sigma\n=\n\\frac32-\\frac1{2h}\n&lt;\n\\frac32.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every independent set of \\(G\\) has cardinality strictly less than \\(n/m\\).", "statement": "<p>Soundness (paper Corollary 7.2).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be the graph of Proposition 1, constructed from \\(\\Phi\\), with \\(n=|V(G)|\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(m\\ge4\\), and let \\(d\\ge4\\) be an even square integer with \\(\\sqrt d&gt;4m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\na=\\frac1{4m},\n\\qquad\nb_0=\\frac1{2m},\n\\qquad\n\\nu=\\frac1{2^7m^3},\n\\qquad\n\\beta=\\frac\\nu8,\n\\qquad\n\\ell=\\frac2\\beta,\n\\qquad\nh=2\\ell,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma=\\frac1{h^2\\ell^2}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n\\[\nh&lt;d,\n\\qquad\n16h+32h^2\\le\\frac{\\nu d}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{val}(\\Phi)\\le\\sigma.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every independent set of \\(G\\) has cardinality strictly less than \\(n/m\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\tau(G)&gt;\\left(1-\\frac1m\\right)n.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose an independent set \\(A\\) has cardinality at least\n\\[\n\\frac n m=2b_0n.\n\\]</p><p>All vertices with \\(\\|S_v\\|_*\\le t\\) form a clique: for any two of them, the triangle inequality gives\n\\[\n\\|S_v+S_w\\|_*\\le2t.\n\\]\nThus at most one vertex of \\(A\\) has norm at most \\(t\\).</p><p>Since\n\\[\nn\\ge p&gt;\\frac1{b_0},\n\\]\nthe number of high-norm vertices in \\(A\\) is at least\n\\[\n2b_0n-1&gt;b_0n.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 3 therefore supplies the single fixed odd Lipschitz function \\(F\\), its variance lower bound, and the three oscillation bounds. Apply Proposition 4 to obtain fixed \\(J,\\mathbf a\\) with retained own-question variance. Proposition 5 then supplies fixed local lists under the remaining product law with\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ\\ge\\frac{\\beta h}{2}=2.\n\\]\nThis contradicts Proposition 6, which gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb EZ&lt;\\frac32\n\\]\nunder the same remaining product law.</p><p>The lower bound averages over hidden seeds and continuous batch weights for the fixed \\(J,\\mathbf a\\). The upper bound fixes further weights and seeds only to decode a single occurrence and is then averaged back. It does not require an energy lower bound, or a derivative identity, at every such further fixing.</p><p>We have ruled out every independent set of cardinality at least \\(n/m\\). The complement of any vertex cover is independent, so every cover has cardinality strictly greater than \\(n-n/m\\), as claimed.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 8, "cite"], [2, 13, "cite"], [2, 16, "cite"], [2, 17, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [8, 17, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 17, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 17, "cite"], [14, 9, "cite"], [14, 10, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"]]});
